    "title": "Floor 2: Communicate Like a Leader",
    "paragraphs": [
      "10. Control the Frame Before Debating the Solution\nThe source argues that whoever defines the problem often shapes the debate.",
      "Consider a team that says, “We need to hire three more people.” A weak discussion immediately becomes an argument about headcount and budget. A stronger leader first asks whether the real problem is capacity, priorities, workflow, or too much low-value work.",
      "The initial framing assumes the solution is more people. The reframed question examines why the current team lacks capacity and whether hiring is the best response.",
      "This is a form of first-principles thinking: separate the problem from the proposed solution.\nA practical reframing sequence\n1. Hear the proposal. What solution is someone suggesting?\n2. Identify the underlying problem. What is not working?\n3. Separate symptoms from causes. What evidence supports the diagnosis?\n4. List possible explanations. Is it capacity, prioritisation, skill, process, tools, or decision delay?\n5. Define the decision. What must be decided now?\n6. Choose the right next step. What information or experiment will resolve uncertainty?\nUseful phrases:\n- “I think that's a separate problem. Let's park it for now.”\n- “Before we decide on the solution, let's define the actual constraint.”\n- “What evidence tells us this is a headcount problem?”\n- “Which part of the process is creating the bottleneck?”\n- “Let's return to the decision we need to make today.”\nControlling the frame should not mean suppressing disagreement. It means keeping the discussion focused on the real issue and making sure relevant evidence is heard.\n11. Speak in Headlines\nA leader should communicate the main point before providing the background.",
      "Long introductions, excessive disclaimers, and meandering explanations make it difficult for a team to identify what matters.",
      "The source calls this cutting words and adding weight.\nWeak communication\n“I've been looking through the reports and I'm not completely sure, but perhaps onboarding could be contributing to churn. Maybe we should talk to more customers and see what we can find out.”",
      "Stronger communication\n“Churn increased this month. Maya will interview ten recently cancelled customers by Thursday, present the three leading causes on Friday, and recommend a corrective action.”",
      "The second version contains:\n- The issue.\n- The owner.\n- The task.\n- The deadline.\n- The expected output.\nIt does not require the speaker to pretend to know more than they do. If the cause is uncertain, say so directly:\n“Churn increased this month. We do not yet know why. Maya will interview ten recently cancelled customers by Thursday and present the three most common causes on Friday.”",
      "Clarity and intellectual honesty belong together.\n12. Use Calm, Deliberate Delivery\nThe source describes a leader's composure as something that influences the emotional tone of a team. In a crisis, people look for someone who can think clearly rather than amplify panic.",
      "Calm does not mean having no emotions. It means regulating how those emotions affect decisions and other people.",
      "Practical behaviours:\n- Slow down enough to be understood.\n- State the facts before speculating.\n- Separate what is known from what is unknown.\n- Avoid blaming people while the situation is still being diagnosed.\n- Identify the immediate priority.\n- Explain the next update or decision point.\n- Do not make promises that cannot be kept.\nThe source uses deliberately provocative language about never crying in business. A more useful leadership principle is that people in positions of responsibility should manage emotional expression so that the team can continue to work safely and effectively. Leaders are human and may experience grief, stress, or distress. Seeking support is not a failure. The key is not to make employees responsible for managing the leader's emotions during a crisis.\n13. End Important Conversations With Three Things\nEvery important meeting or conversation should end with:\n1. A decision: What was agreed?\n2. An owner: Who is responsible?\n3. A date: When is it due?\nWithout these, a meeting can create the feeling of progress without producing action.\nExample close\n“We are choosing option two. Sarah owns the implementation. The first version is due Thursday at 3:00 p.m., and we will review it Friday morning. Is everyone clear on the decision and responsibilities?”",
      "Then record it in the team's task system or written notes.",
      "A meeting summary should include:\n- Decision.\n- Owner.\n- Deliverable.\n- Deadline.\n- Dependencies.\n- Review point.\nAvoid vague endings such as “Great discussion,” “Let's keep moving,” or “Someone should follow up.”\n14. Lead From Below: Bring Options, Not Just Problems\nLeadership is not limited to people with formal titles. Employees can demonstrate judgement and earn trust by bringing structured recommendations to their managers.",
      "Instead of saying:\n“We have a huge problem. What should we do?”",
      "Bring:\n- The problem and evidence.\n- The options available.\n- Your recommendation.\n- The reason for that recommendation.\n- The risks and trade-offs.\n- The decision or support you need.\nA concise format:\nProblem: [What is happening, supported by evidence.]\nOptions: [Two or three realistic choices.]\nRecommendation: [What I think we should do.]\nReason: [Why this option is strongest.]\nRisk: [What could go wrong and how we would respond.]\nDecision needed: [What I need from you.]",
      "This reduces the amount of diagnosis the manager must do before they can help.\n15. Take Ownership When Things Go Wrong\nThe source strongly emphasises ownership. A leader should not hide behind “I don't know who did that” or use a chain of explanations to protect their ego.",
      "A useful ownership statement has four parts:\n1. Acknowledge the outcome.\n2. State your part in the failure.\n3. Explain the correction.\n4. Assign the next action and review.\nExample:\n“I approved the plan and underestimated the risk. That decision contributed to the problem. Sarah will own the immediate correction, and I will review the process with her by Friday to prevent a repeat.”",
      "Ownership does not mean falsely accepting blame for everything or ignoring misconduct. It means accepting responsibility for the decisions, systems, and oversight within your remit.",
      "The source's point is that people can often forgive a bad decision. Trust is damaged when a leader distorts the facts to protect their image."
    ]
