  closingParagraphs: [
  "Final Self-Assessment: Which Floor Are You On?\nUse these questions to identify the most important leadership skill to develop next.\nLobby — Leadership beliefs\n- Do I avoid difficult conversations because I want to be liked?\n- Do I put decisions to a vote when someone needs to own the call?\n- Do I rescue recurring problems instead of building prevention?\n- Do I confuse care for employees with avoiding accountability?\n- Am I still trying to be the hardest worker in every function?\nFloor 1 — Delegation\n- Are my instructions specific and measurable?\n- Do people have the resources and authority to deliver?\n- Do I explain why the work matters?\n- Are deadlines exact?\n- Do I coach people through imperfect work instead of automatically taking it back?\nFloor 2 — Communication\n- Do I define the real problem before debating solutions?\n- Do I state the main point early?\n- Can I remain composed when the team is under pressure?\n- Do important conversations end with a decision, owner, and date?\n- Do I bring options and recommendations when escalating problems?\n- Do I accept responsibility for my decisions?\nFloor 3 — Hiring\n- Have I defined what success in the role actually requires?\n- Do I evaluate evidence rather than interview charm?\n- Do I understand the candidate's experience with the actual problem?\n- Do I assess industry and scale fit?\n- Do I maintain a candidate pipeline instead of hiring only when desperate?\n- Do I use relevant work samples and informed references?\nFloor 4 — Performance and retention\n- Are incentives connected to meaningful outcomes?\n- Do I protect employees' time?\n- Is recognition specific and timely?\n- Do strong performers receive meaningful responsibility and access?\n- Are standards enforced fairly and consistently?\nPenthouse — Ownership\n- Am I focused on the decisions that matter most?\n- Can the team explain the company's direction?\n- Do my actions match the standards I expect?\n- Can important work continue without my daily involvement?\n- Are critical processes documented and backed up?\n- Is the business becoming less dependent on me over time?\nConclusion: The Goal Is to Build a Business That Can Climb Without You\nLeadership is not simply confidence, charisma, authority, or working harder than everyone else. It is the ability to create clarity, make decisions, develop people, protect standards, and build systems that produce results consistently.",
  "The progression is practical:\n- Stop chasing approval and start earning trust.\n- Stop rescuing every problem and teach people to own outcomes.\n- Stop giving vague instructions and define successful work.\n- Stop allowing meetings to drift and turn conversations into decisions.\n- Stop hiring based on charm and evaluate evidence.\n- Stop rewarding activity alone and recognise meaningful contribution.\n- Stop wasting the time of strong people and give them room to do important work.\n- Stop keeping the business in your head and build systems that distribute knowledge and authority.\nThe elevator is not about reaching a title. It is about changing how the organisation operates as its complexity grows.",
  "At the beginning, the founder may need to do almost everything. At the next stage, the leader must help others do the work well. At the highest stage, the owner builds a company in which capable people can make sound decisions, execute the mission, and keep important systems running without constant intervention.",
  "The measure of leadership is not how much the organisation needs you every day. It is how well the organisation performs because of the people and systems you have built."
]
